1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:01,890
Right now, somewhere on Earth,

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:05,610
a dish the size of a football field is pointed at an empty patch of sky,

3
00:00:06,070 --> 00:00:08,730
listening for a signal that takes over 20 hours to arrive.

4
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:12,830
The thing sending it is 15 billion miles away.

5
00:00:13,230 --> 00:00:15,110
It was built in the 1970s.

6
00:00:15,530 --> 00:00:17,090
And this year, on purpose,

7
00:00:17,890 --> 00:00:19,410
NASA is turning parts of it off.

8
00:00:20,790 --> 00:00:24,050
It's called Voyager 1. NASA built it to last five years.

9
00:00:25,290 --> 00:00:27,130
It has been running for 49.

10
00:00:28,190 --> 00:00:33,430
It has survived a 1970s chip failure nobody alive today knew how to fix at first.

11
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:37,210
It has crossed a boundary no human -made object had ever crossed,

12
00:00:37,390 --> 00:00:41,010
and its power source is dying on a schedule nobody can stop.

13
00:00:42,270 --> 00:00:45,670
This is the story of the two machines that outlived everyone who built them,

14
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:49,270
and the slow, deliberate way NASA is now saying goodbye.

15
00:00:50,670 --> 00:00:51,850
You're watching Mindwide.

16
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:55,990
This is the story of Voyager 1 and Voyager 2,

17
00:00:55,990 --> 00:00:57,810
built for a five -year mission,

18
00:00:58,430 --> 00:01:00,210
still flying after 49 years,

19
00:01:00,550 --> 00:01:02,770
and now, one instrument at a time,

20
00:01:02,970 --> 00:01:08,030
going quiet. Chapter 1, the machine that was never supposed to see this

21
00:01:08,030 --> 00:01:13,170
year. In 1965, an MIT graduate student noticed something

22
00:01:13,170 --> 00:01:15,730
strange. Around the year 1977,

23
00:01:16,330 --> 00:01:21,350
Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune would line up in a way that let a single

24
00:01:21,350 --> 00:01:22,970
spacecraft visit all four,

25
00:01:22,970 --> 00:01:26,690
using each planet's own gravity as a slingshot to the next.

26
00:01:27,970 --> 00:01:31,710
That alignment happens roughly once every 175 years.

27
00:01:32,370 --> 00:01:34,050
NASA called the idea the Grand Tour.

28
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:38,430
In January of 1972, the Grand Tour was cancelled.

29
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:40,810
The money went to the space shuttle instead.

30
00:01:42,090 --> 00:01:43,570
What survived was a smaller,

31
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:48,210
cheaper version. Two identical spacecraft aimed at just two planets,

32
00:01:48,590 --> 00:01:52,810
Jupiter and Saturn. NASA called it Mariner -Jupiter -Saturn 77.

33
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:56,350
Even that cheaper version wasn't a sure thing.

34
00:01:56,950 --> 00:02:00,590
JPL brought three different budget options to Congress in early 1972,

35
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:04,630
ranging from about $210 million up to $320 million.

36
00:02:06,090 --> 00:02:09,650
On March 7, 1977, months before launch,

37
00:02:10,110 --> 00:02:11,850
NASA's administrator renamed the mission.

38
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:14,330
Mariner -Jupiter -Saturn became Voyager.

39
00:02:15,830 --> 00:02:18,270
One man would stay with this mission for the rest of his career.

40
00:02:18,270 --> 00:02:22,210
Ed Stone became Voyager's project scientist in 1972,

41
00:02:22,810 --> 00:02:26,730
and he was still its project scientist 50 years later in 2022.

42
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:31,290
50 years, one person, one job.

43
00:02:31,730 --> 00:02:34,270
He died in June of 2024 at 88,

44
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:38,310
having outlived almost every deadline anyone ever set for this mission.

45
00:02:39,710 --> 00:02:41,210
Here is the number that matters most.

46
00:02:41,870 --> 00:02:43,750
NASA's own mission documents say it plainly.

47
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:46,490
The spacecraft were built to last five years,

48
00:02:46,490 --> 00:02:48,510
long enough to reach Jupiter and Saturn,

49
00:02:48,930 --> 00:02:54,480
and nothing more. Nobody designing it in 1977 expected

50
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:57,380
anyone to still be listening to it in 2026.

51
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:02,840
Confusingly, Voyager 2 left Earth first on August 20,

52
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:08,320
1977. Voyager 1 launched second on September 5th on a

53
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:12,620
faster, more direct path that would let it arrive at Jupiter months earlier.

54
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:18,340
Both rode Titan 3E Centaur rockets off the same launch pad in Cape Canaveral,

55
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:20,420
Florida, three weeks apart.

56
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:23,640
The two spacecraft weren't just similar.

57
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:25,300
They were identical twins,

58
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:28,320
built side by side, so that if one failed outright,

59
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:30,660
the other could still complete the mission alone.

60
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:34,380
That backup plan is exactly what ended up happening,

61
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:36,220
just not the way anyone expected.

62
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:41,020
Both machines survived. It's the plan itself that eventually had to change.

63
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:46,940
Almost nobody talks about how close Voyager 1 came to never leaving the atmosphere at

64
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:49,580
all. During its own launch,

65
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:54,360
the Titan rocket's first stage burned an off -ratio fuel mixture and shut down early.

66
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:56,860
The Centaur upper stage had to compensate,

67
00:03:57,320 --> 00:03:59,380
burning harder than planned to make up the difference.

68
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:03,480
By the time Voyager 1 reached a stable trajectory,

69
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:07,280
the Centaur stage had roughly three and a half seconds of fuel left.

70
00:04:08,990 --> 00:04:10,090
Three and a half seconds.

71
00:04:10,090 --> 00:04:14,550
That was the entire margin between the most photographed spacecraft in NASA's history

72
00:04:14,550 --> 00:04:16,690
and a mission that ends on the launch pad.

73
00:04:18,130 --> 00:04:22,470
Inside both spacecraft sat six computers in three redundant pairs,

74
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:26,590
running on a combined clock speed of about 8 ,000 instructions per second.

75
00:04:28,070 --> 00:04:29,650
NASA has run its own comparison.

76
00:04:30,070 --> 00:04:34,990
A single smartphone from 2013 could process over 14 billion instructions per second.

77
00:04:35,230 --> 00:04:36,770
That's not a difference in years.

78
00:04:37,230 --> 00:04:38,350
That's a difference in kind.

79
00:04:40,090 --> 00:04:44,010
Power for both spacecraft comes from three plutonium generators each,

80
00:04:44,410 --> 00:04:48,010
converting the heat of radioactive decay directly into electricity,

81
00:04:48,370 --> 00:04:51,470
at launch that produced about 470 watts,

82
00:04:52,070 --> 00:04:53,910
barely enough to run a couple of hairdryers.

83
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:58,410
That decay never stops, and it never reverses.

84
00:04:58,990 --> 00:05:02,810
Every year, both spacecraft have a little less power than the year before,

85
00:05:03,050 --> 00:05:07,390
permanently. That single fact is the reason this whole story exists.

86
00:05:08,950 --> 00:05:11,590
To put 470 watts in perspective,

87
00:05:12,070 --> 00:05:16,150
that's roughly what it takes to run four or five ordinary household light bulbs.

88
00:05:16,710 --> 00:05:21,050
An entire spacecraft 15 billion miles from the nearest wall outlet,

89
00:05:21,290 --> 00:05:23,510
running on about as much power as your living room lamp.

90
00:05:25,050 --> 00:05:27,970
Chapter two, the accident that became a discovery.

91
00:05:29,490 --> 00:05:34,490
On March 5th, 1979, Voyager 1 made its closest approach to Jupiter,

92
00:05:34,490 --> 00:05:38,350
passing within about 350 ,000 kilometers of the cloud tops.

93
00:05:39,790 --> 00:05:44,350
It took almost 19 ,000 pictures of the Jupiter system and found something nobody had

94
00:05:44,350 --> 00:05:47,530
confirmed before. Jupiter has its own faint ring.

95
00:05:48,790 --> 00:05:51,210
Two NASA probes, Pioneer 10 and 11,

96
00:05:51,390 --> 00:05:53,970
had already flown past Jupiter earlier in the decade,

97
00:05:54,310 --> 00:05:56,070
but their cameras were far simpler.

98
00:05:56,590 --> 00:05:57,770
Next to Voyager's pictures,

99
00:05:58,310 --> 00:05:59,770
pioneers looked like rough sketches.

100
00:06:00,930 --> 00:06:05,450
Voyager 1's images are also credited with turning up two more small moons hiding close

101
00:06:05,450 --> 00:06:08,390
to Jupiter itself, later named Metis and Thebe.

102
00:06:09,530 --> 00:06:13,250
But the discovery that mattered most almost didn't happen on purpose at all.

103
00:06:14,610 --> 00:06:15,770
Four days after the flyby,

104
00:06:16,050 --> 00:06:19,690
a JPL navigation engineer named Linda Morabito was doing routine work,

105
00:06:19,970 --> 00:06:21,830
enhancing a picture of Jupiter's moon Io,

106
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:25,330
just to pick out faint background stars for tracking the spacecraft's position.

107
00:06:26,310 --> 00:06:30,070
On the edge of Io, she saw something that shouldn't have been there,

108
00:06:30,350 --> 00:06:35,270
an umbrella -shaped cloud rising roughly 270 kilometers off the moon's surface.

109
00:06:37,010 --> 00:06:40,490
Her team spent the rest of that day ruling out every boring explanation,

110
00:06:40,870 --> 00:06:44,310
a camera glitch, an undiscovered moon passing behind Io.

111
00:06:44,550 --> 00:06:49,750
None of it fit. What she'd actually found was an active volcano erupting

112
00:06:49,750 --> 00:06:52,450
on another world. It was later named Pele.

113
00:06:52,450 --> 00:06:56,810
It was the first time anyone had ever seen active volcanism happening anywhere but Earth.

114
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:01,730
By the time scientists finished reviewing Voyager 1's full image set of Io,

115
00:07:02,130 --> 00:07:03,610
they'd found eight active plumes,

116
00:07:03,850 --> 00:07:08,010
not one. A woman doing routine navigation math,

117
00:07:08,150 --> 00:07:09,470
looking for background stars,

118
00:07:09,490 --> 00:07:13,750
had just proven that Earth wasn't the only geologically alive world in the solar system.

119
00:07:15,370 --> 00:07:17,590
Planetary scientists have been teaching that discovery,

120
00:07:17,790 --> 00:07:21,930
and the way it happened to every generation of students who came after her.

121
00:07:23,170 --> 00:07:27,630
Voyager 1's cameras also caught Jupiter's great red spot as something dynamic for the

122
00:07:27,630 --> 00:07:30,450
first time, a storm large enough to swallow Earth,

123
00:07:30,810 --> 00:07:32,150
rotating counterclockwise,

124
00:07:32,490 --> 00:07:35,130
tangled up with smaller storms feeding in and out of it.

125
00:07:36,490 --> 00:07:40,370
Jupiter had just stopped being a picture in a textbook and started being a place with

126
00:07:40,370 --> 00:07:43,750
weather. And Jupiter was only the first stop.

127
00:07:44,410 --> 00:07:46,850
Both spacecrafts still had a much longer road ahead,

128
00:07:47,070 --> 00:07:50,530
and one of them was about to make a choice that would end its journey early.

129
00:07:54,950 --> 00:07:59,610
On November 12th, 1980, Voyager 1 reached Saturn,

130
00:08:00,050 --> 00:08:03,330
passing about 124 ,000 kilometers from the planet.

131
00:08:04,870 --> 00:08:08,350
It found strange radial spoke patterns moving through Saturn's rings,

132
00:08:08,910 --> 00:08:12,870
five new moons, and a level of structure in the rings nobody had modeled before.

133
00:08:14,390 --> 00:08:16,370
But mission planners had a decision to make,

134
00:08:16,670 --> 00:08:18,210
and it came down to one moon,

135
00:08:18,850 --> 00:08:21,990
Titan. Titan had a thick atmosphere,

136
00:08:22,550 --> 00:08:26,630
mostly nitrogen, so dense that Voyager's cameras couldn't see through it to the surface

137
00:08:26,630 --> 00:08:30,330
at all. That single, frustrating,

138
00:08:30,730 --> 00:08:35,210
opaque haze is exactly why later missions went back with radar instead of cameras.

139
00:08:35,810 --> 00:08:37,490
Voyager didn't just study Titan,

140
00:08:37,890 --> 00:08:41,230
it's the reason anyone thought Titan was worth another trip at all.

141
00:08:42,610 --> 00:08:46,090
Engineers had studied more than 10 ,000 possible trajectories,

142
00:08:46,090 --> 00:08:49,830
a close pass by Titan to study that atmosphere properly,

143
00:08:50,270 --> 00:08:53,270
and a path onward to Uranus were mutually exclusive.

144
00:08:54,190 --> 00:08:55,290
Voyager 1 could only do one.

145
00:08:56,770 --> 00:09:01,710
NASA chose Titan. The flyby bent Voyager 1's path permanently out of the plane the planets

146
00:09:01,710 --> 00:09:04,430
orbit in. It would never encounter another planet again.

147
00:09:06,290 --> 00:09:11,570
Voyager 1's entire future beyond Saturn was traded for one close look at a single moon's

148
00:09:11,570 --> 00:09:15,730
clouds. Voyager 2 had been launched on a different path,

149
00:09:16,090 --> 00:09:18,770
specifically to keep the door to Uranus and Neptune open.

150
00:09:19,430 --> 00:09:21,010
Now it would have to make that journey alone.

151
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:26,990
On August 25, 1981, Voyager 2 made its own pass by Saturn,

152
00:09:27,530 --> 00:09:29,810
and about a hundred minutes after closest approach,

153
00:09:30,210 --> 00:09:31,590
something on board seized up.

154
00:09:32,750 --> 00:09:35,150
The gearing that aimed its camera platform jammed,

155
00:09:35,470 --> 00:09:39,750
from a lubrication failure identical to one that had already hit Voyager 1 at Jupiter.

156
00:09:39,750 --> 00:09:44,070
For several days, Voyager 2 couldn't reliably point its own cameras.

157
00:09:45,590 --> 00:09:50,430
Engineers 1 ,500 million kilometers away had to diagnose and fix a mechanical jam on

158
00:09:50,430 --> 00:09:51,870
a machine they could not touch,

159
00:09:52,190 --> 00:09:56,110
without losing the one trajectory that still led to two undiscovered planets.

160
00:09:57,650 --> 00:10:00,050
By August 28, three days later,

161
00:10:00,310 --> 00:10:01,370
they had it working again.

162
00:10:01,950 --> 00:10:04,390
The path to Uranus and Neptune was still open.

163
00:10:05,750 --> 00:10:08,130
One spacecraft's journey had just ended at Saturn.

164
00:10:08,130 --> 00:10:10,710
The others was about to go somewhere no machine,

165
00:10:11,170 --> 00:10:13,130
before or since, has ever gone.

166
00:10:14,730 --> 00:10:19,410
Chapter 4, The Only Spacecraft On January 24,

167
00:10:19,850 --> 00:10:24,930
1986, Voyager 2 flew within about 81 ,000 kilometers of Uranus'

168
00:10:25,050 --> 00:10:28,490
cloud tops. It found 11 new moons,

169
00:10:28,950 --> 00:10:31,590
two new rings, and something genuinely strange.

170
00:10:32,310 --> 00:10:36,290
Uranus' magnetic field is tilted about 59 degrees off its spin axis,

171
00:10:36,290 --> 00:10:38,110
and isn't even centered on the planet.

172
00:10:39,470 --> 00:10:41,790
No other planet studied works quite like that.

173
00:10:42,130 --> 00:10:46,710
As of today, in 2026, no other spacecraft has ever been back to check.

174
00:10:48,070 --> 00:10:49,290
Three and a half years later,

175
00:10:49,510 --> 00:10:53,570
on August 25, 1989, Voyager 2 reached Neptune,

176
00:10:54,070 --> 00:10:56,710
coming within about 4 ,800 kilometers of the clouds.

177
00:10:57,210 --> 00:11:00,250
It remains the closest planetary approach of the entire mission.

178
00:11:01,590 --> 00:11:05,710
That night, JPL turned its own TV studio into Neptune Mission Control.

179
00:11:05,710 --> 00:11:07,950
Engineers crowded around monitors,

180
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:11,370
watching pictures arrive after a four -hour trip at the speed of light.

181
00:11:12,790 --> 00:11:15,350
What came back was a storm called the Great Dark Spot,

182
00:11:15,650 --> 00:11:18,210
with winds measured at over 2 ,000 kilometers an hour,

183
00:11:18,490 --> 00:11:20,990
the fastest ever recorded anywhere in the Solar System.

184
00:11:22,710 --> 00:11:23,910
And then there was Triton,

185
00:11:24,230 --> 00:11:28,530
Neptune's largest moon, one of the coldest surfaces ever measured in the Solar System,

186
00:11:28,850 --> 00:11:31,470
at about minus 235 degrees Celsius.

187
00:11:32,550 --> 00:11:34,030
It should have been dead.

188
00:11:34,630 --> 00:11:37,730
Instead, Voyager 2 found active nitrogen geysers,

189
00:11:38,110 --> 00:11:40,630
dark plumes climbing 8 kilometers into the sky,

190
00:11:40,930 --> 00:11:43,770
drifting 150 kilometers downwind before settling.

191
00:11:45,410 --> 00:11:46,830
The morning after the encounter,

192
00:11:47,230 --> 00:11:52,270
the sitting Vice President of the United States showed up at JPL personally to congratulate

193
00:11:52,270 --> 00:11:55,010
the team. As of this year,

194
00:11:55,370 --> 00:11:59,750
Voyager 2 remains the only spacecraft in history to have ever flown past either Uranus

195
00:11:59,750 --> 00:12:04,810
or Neptune. NASA has a concept for a future mission to

196
00:12:04,810 --> 00:12:07,190
Uranus. It has no approved launch date,

197
00:12:07,550 --> 00:12:11,850
and realistically wouldn't arrive until sometime in the 2030s at the earliest.

198
00:12:13,370 --> 00:12:15,590
For Neptune, there isn't even a concept.

199
00:12:16,150 --> 00:12:18,250
No mission of any kind is currently planned.

200
00:12:19,570 --> 00:12:20,950
For at least another decade,

201
00:12:21,290 --> 00:12:26,350
and possibly longer, everything humanity knows firsthand about two entire planets

202
00:12:26,350 --> 00:12:30,550
still comes from one spacecraft's single pass in the 1980s.

203
00:12:31,510 --> 00:12:34,490
The Grand Tour was over, but for both spacecraft,

204
00:12:35,070 --> 00:12:37,870
the most human part of the mission hadn't happened yet.

205
00:12:42,270 --> 00:12:44,430
Before either spacecraft launched,

206
00:12:44,970 --> 00:12:47,510
NASA gave a small committee an unusual assignment.

207
00:12:48,090 --> 00:12:49,950
Build a message for whoever might find it,

208
00:12:50,270 --> 00:12:55,230
someday, out there. The committee was chaired by astronomer Carl Sagan.

209
00:12:55,830 --> 00:12:57,510
Alongside him, Frank Drake,

210
00:12:57,950 --> 00:13:00,790
artist John Lomburg, music producer Timothy Ferris,

211
00:13:01,050 --> 00:13:02,670
and a young writer named Ann Druyn.

212
00:13:04,010 --> 00:13:09,250
On June 3rd, 1977, two days after Sagan and Druyn privately told

213
00:13:09,250 --> 00:13:10,210
each other they were in love,

214
00:13:10,770 --> 00:13:15,550
Druyn sat down and recorded her own brainwaves and heartbeat to be encoded onto the record.

215
00:13:15,950 --> 00:13:17,610
She was thinking about him the whole time.

216
00:13:19,250 --> 00:13:22,130
Neither of them knew yet whether the record would even be approved.

217
00:13:22,710 --> 00:13:27,130
It was days later. What they built was a 12 -inch,

218
00:13:27,590 --> 00:13:28,730
gold -plated copper disc,

219
00:13:28,970 --> 00:13:31,050
packed with a needle and cartridge to play it,

220
00:13:31,310 --> 00:13:33,070
sealed in a protective aluminum jacket.

221
00:13:34,690 --> 00:13:37,250
On it, 115 images of Earth in its life,

222
00:13:37,830 --> 00:13:39,710
spoken greetings in 55 languages,

223
00:13:40,090 --> 00:13:43,150
opening with Acadian, a language 6 ,000 years dead,

224
00:13:43,410 --> 00:13:45,290
and closing with a dialect of Chinese.

225
00:13:47,170 --> 00:13:49,150
And 90 minutes of music, Bach,

226
00:13:49,870 --> 00:13:52,810
Mozart, Beethoven, Chuck Berry's Johnny B.

227
00:13:53,290 --> 00:13:55,730
Goode, a blues recording by Blind Willie Johnson,

228
00:13:56,170 --> 00:13:58,750
made in the 1920s, folk music from Peru,

229
00:13:59,190 --> 00:14:04,390
New Guinea, and Senegal. The record's cover is a diagram showing how to play

230
00:14:04,390 --> 00:14:08,630
it and where Earth is, marked using the positions of 14 pulsars,

231
00:14:09,050 --> 00:14:11,690
cosmic lighthouses whose blink rate works like a timestamp.

232
00:14:13,310 --> 00:14:15,610
Even the diagram's clock isn't measured in seconds.

233
00:14:16,010 --> 00:14:18,470
It's built on a single tick of the hydrogen atom,

234
00:14:18,470 --> 00:14:20,490
the most common element in the universe,

235
00:14:20,970 --> 00:14:25,070
so that any civilization advanced enough to find the record would already recognize the

236
00:14:25,070 --> 00:14:29,350
unit being used. Here's what almost nobody explains clearly.

237
00:14:29,630 --> 00:14:32,410
This was never meant as a message anyone would actually receive.

238
00:14:33,950 --> 00:14:35,750
In roughly 40 ,000 years,

239
00:14:36,110 --> 00:14:41,130
Voyager 1 will pass within about 1 .7 light years of a star called Gliese 445,

240
00:14:42,010 --> 00:14:43,650
not because that star is close now,

241
00:14:43,910 --> 00:14:46,090
but because of how both objects happen to be moving.

242
00:14:47,650 --> 00:14:49,470
Sagan himself said it plainly,

243
00:14:49,950 --> 00:14:53,970
the record would only ever be found and played if there are spacefaring civilizations

244
00:14:53,970 --> 00:14:56,330
out there in interstellar space to find it.

245
00:14:56,610 --> 00:15:00,730
He wasn't being modest. He knew exactly how unlikely that was.

246
00:15:02,010 --> 00:15:04,790
It is, functionally, a message in a bottle,

247
00:15:05,130 --> 00:15:08,410
thrown into an ocean with no shore for 40 ,000 years.

248
00:15:09,770 --> 00:15:11,630
There's a story that gets repeated a lot,

249
00:15:11,870 --> 00:15:16,590
that the Beatles' Here Comes the Sun was blocked from the record over a copyright dispute.

250
00:15:17,270 --> 00:15:19,910
The record's own music producer has said that's not true.

251
00:15:20,250 --> 00:15:23,290
The song was never seriously being considered in the first place.

252
00:15:24,890 --> 00:15:26,730
What's real is stranger than the rumor.

253
00:15:27,230 --> 00:15:32,350
A committee of a handful of people working in 1977 decided what would represent

254
00:15:32,350 --> 00:15:36,990
every human being who has ever lived to anyone who might someday find it.

255
00:15:37,910 --> 00:15:40,930
That record is still bolted to the outside of Voyager 1.

256
00:15:40,930 --> 00:15:44,850
Right now, 15 billion miles from the people who made it.

257
00:15:46,370 --> 00:15:49,110
Nobody has played that exact record since the day it launched.

258
00:15:49,650 --> 00:15:51,610
Only test copies, kept on the ground,

259
00:15:51,930 --> 00:15:53,390
have ever actually been listened to.

260
00:15:54,650 --> 00:15:57,610
But before Voyager 1 carried that record into the dark for good,

261
00:15:58,090 --> 00:16:00,190
it did one more thing for the people back home.

262
00:16:00,750 --> 00:16:03,530
It looked back. Chapter 6.

263
00:16:04,110 --> 00:16:05,990
The photograph Sagan had to fight for.

264
00:16:07,150 --> 00:16:11,710
By 1990, Voyager 1's planetary mission had been over for almost a decade.

265
00:16:12,130 --> 00:16:15,270
Its cameras were about to be shut off for good to save power.

266
00:16:15,950 --> 00:16:17,550
Carl Sagan had one request left.

267
00:16:19,210 --> 00:16:22,390
He wanted the spacecraft to turn back and take one picture of home.

268
00:16:23,170 --> 00:16:25,370
NASA's own engineers pushed back for years,

269
00:16:25,730 --> 00:16:28,130
worried about pointing the camera anywhere near the sun.

270
00:16:29,410 --> 00:16:34,170
It took eight years and six separate requests before Sagan got his answer.

271
00:16:35,490 --> 00:16:40,650
On February 14th, 1990, from about 3 .7 billion miles

272
00:16:40,650 --> 00:16:45,890
away, Voyager 1 turned its camera back toward the inner solar system and took 60 pictures,

273
00:16:46,210 --> 00:16:48,310
forming what NASA called the family portrait.

274
00:16:49,610 --> 00:16:53,390
Neptune, Uranus, Saturn, Jupiter,

275
00:16:54,030 --> 00:16:56,430
Venus, and Earth. Mercury,

276
00:16:57,110 --> 00:17:02,190
Mars, and Pluto were simply too faint or too close to the sun's glare to show up at

277
00:17:02,190 --> 00:17:05,610
all. In one of those frames sits Earth,

278
00:17:06,270 --> 00:17:08,050
less than a single pixel wide.

279
00:17:09,830 --> 00:17:11,970
There's a stripe of light running through the frame,

280
00:17:12,290 --> 00:17:14,830
and people often assume that's sunlight falling on Earth.

281
00:17:15,430 --> 00:17:20,190
It isn't. It's scattered light bouncing around inside the camera itself from shooting

282
00:17:20,190 --> 00:17:24,330
this close to the sun. Earth just happened to land inside it.

283
00:17:25,230 --> 00:17:29,070
A coincidence turned it into the most famous photograph of Earth ever taken.

284
00:17:30,170 --> 00:17:32,750
This wasn't the first photograph of Earth from space.

285
00:17:33,170 --> 00:17:34,970
That goes back to the 1940s.

286
00:17:35,350 --> 00:17:39,650
It was the first time humanity had ever seen itself from the edge of its own solar system.

287
00:17:41,170 --> 00:17:43,450
Sagan later wrote the words that made the photo famous.

288
00:17:43,850 --> 00:17:46,810
His own, exact sentence. Look again at that dot.

289
00:17:47,170 --> 00:17:48,630
That's here. That's home.

290
00:17:48,990 --> 00:17:52,410
That's us. On it, everyone you love,

291
00:17:52,830 --> 00:17:55,230
everyone you know, everyone you ever heard of,

292
00:17:55,230 --> 00:17:57,410
every human being, whoever was,

293
00:17:57,770 --> 00:18:02,830
lived out their lives. Every saint and sinner in the history of our

294
00:18:02,830 --> 00:18:05,710
species lived there on a moat of dust,

295
00:18:06,010 --> 00:18:10,610
suspended in a sunbeam. With that photo taken,

296
00:18:11,070 --> 00:18:12,670
Voyager 1's cameras went dark,

297
00:18:12,910 --> 00:18:16,630
permanently, to save what little power was left for the journey still ahead.

298
00:18:18,350 --> 00:18:20,710
Chapter 7. The edge that isn't an edge.

299
00:18:22,050 --> 00:18:23,750
For decades after the photograph,

300
00:18:23,750 --> 00:18:26,030
both spacecraft kept flying outward,

301
00:18:26,270 --> 00:18:31,250
past the planets, past anything the sun's light could meaningfully reach toward a boundary

302
00:18:31,250 --> 00:18:32,910
nobody had ever actually measured.

303
00:18:34,050 --> 00:18:35,530
It's called the heliopause,

304
00:18:35,850 --> 00:18:40,410
the place where the pressure of the sun's own solar wind finally gives out against the

305
00:18:40,410 --> 00:18:41,950
pressure of the wider galaxy.

306
00:18:43,350 --> 00:18:44,990
There is no wall out there,

307
00:18:45,250 --> 00:18:47,930
no line to see, no camera could have caught it,

308
00:18:47,930 --> 00:18:50,250
because Voyager 1's cameras were already off.

309
00:18:51,730 --> 00:18:53,470
So NASA measured it a different way.

310
00:18:53,750 --> 00:18:58,570
Voyager 1's instruments detected a sudden jump in the density of charged particles around

311
00:18:58,570 --> 00:19:03,350
the spacecraft, triggered by a burst of activity from the sun that let the plasma wave

312
00:19:03,350 --> 00:19:05,610
instrument essentially hear the difference.

313
00:19:07,110 --> 00:19:09,610
That crossing happened on August 25th,

314
00:19:09,710 --> 00:19:12,690
2012, about 11 billion miles from the sun.

315
00:19:13,490 --> 00:19:15,430
NASA didn't announce it until over a year later,

316
00:19:15,450 --> 00:19:17,250
once the data was fully confirmed.

317
00:19:19,030 --> 00:19:22,910
Voyager 2 crossed its own version of that same boundary on November 5th,

318
00:19:22,910 --> 00:19:26,490
2018, and this time its plasma instrument was still working,

319
00:19:26,770 --> 00:19:28,630
so the confirmation came almost immediately.

320
00:19:30,130 --> 00:19:32,430
Every headline called this leaving the solar system.

321
00:19:32,710 --> 00:19:34,390
That's not quite right, and it matters.

322
00:19:35,870 --> 00:19:38,630
Both spacecraft are still gravitationally bound to the sun.

323
00:19:39,090 --> 00:19:41,210
They're still inside the outer edge of the Oort cloud,

324
00:19:41,570 --> 00:19:43,250
the sun's own sphere of influence,

325
00:19:43,530 --> 00:19:48,010
which they won't even reach for another 300 years and won't fully clear for another 30

326
00:19:48,010 --> 00:19:51,690
,000. They crossed into interstellar space.

327
00:19:52,210 --> 00:19:57,390
They didn't leave home. NASA had already sent two other spacecraft out ahead of them,

328
00:19:58,070 --> 00:20:00,710
Pioneer 10 and 11, in the early 1970s.

329
00:20:01,150 --> 00:20:02,570
Both went silent years ago,

330
00:20:02,870 --> 00:20:05,890
one in 1995, the other in 2003.

331
00:20:07,450 --> 00:20:11,750
Both Voyages have already outlasted every other deep space mission NASA has ever flown,

332
00:20:12,150 --> 00:20:14,730
and in 2026, they're still not done.

333
00:20:15,970 --> 00:20:20,230
Two machines built for a five -year trip to two planets had just become the only human

334
00:20:20,230 --> 00:20:23,810
-made objects ever confirmed to be traveling through the space between stars.

335
00:20:25,350 --> 00:20:27,890
None of these discoveries would ever reach us at all,

336
00:20:28,150 --> 00:20:30,850
without something built specifically to listen this far.

337
00:20:32,410 --> 00:20:34,150
NASA calls it the Deep Space Network.

338
00:20:34,850 --> 00:20:37,190
Giant dish antennas grouped into three complexes,

339
00:20:37,570 --> 00:20:39,550
one in California, one in Spain,

340
00:20:40,030 --> 00:20:42,290
one in Australia, spaced out across the globe.

341
00:20:43,610 --> 00:20:45,230
That spacing isn't decorative.

342
00:20:45,230 --> 00:20:48,290
As the Earth spins underneath both spacecraft,

343
00:20:48,810 --> 00:20:52,330
at least one of those three complexes always has a clear line of sight,

344
00:20:52,350 --> 00:20:54,590
so the connection never actually has to break.

345
00:20:55,890 --> 00:20:58,770
By the time Voyager 1's signal reaches one of those dishes today,

346
00:20:59,050 --> 00:21:00,310
it has already been traveling,

347
00:21:00,530 --> 00:21:02,790
nonstop, for more than 22 hours.

348
00:21:04,650 --> 00:21:07,150
Every command NASA has ever sent this mission,

349
00:21:07,390 --> 00:21:11,750
and every answer that ever came back rode on that same network of dishes,

350
00:21:11,970 --> 00:21:15,090
built decades before anyone could guess where this would end up.

351
00:21:16,050 --> 00:21:18,150
And still, every single day,

352
00:21:18,530 --> 00:21:19,810
they kept sending data home.

353
00:21:20,250 --> 00:21:24,250
Until one day, in 2023, one of them stopped making sense.

354
00:21:25,950 --> 00:21:28,870
Chapter 8, the glitch nobody alive could fully explain.

355
00:21:30,250 --> 00:21:35,270
On November 14, 2023, Voyager 1 started sending back data that

356
00:21:35,270 --> 00:21:37,330
made no sense, not silence,

357
00:21:37,870 --> 00:21:42,210
something worse, a signal arriving on schedule that was just noise.

358
00:21:43,190 --> 00:21:46,230
For five months, that's all anyone on Earth got,

359
00:21:46,870 --> 00:21:51,690
repeating gibberish from a spacecraft that was clearly still alive but couldn't say anything

360
00:21:51,690 --> 00:21:56,890
that meant anything. Engineers eventually traced it to one failed computer chip

361
00:21:56,890 --> 00:21:58,770
inside the flight data subsystem,

362
00:21:59,170 --> 00:22:01,710
holding roughly 3 % of that system's memory,

363
00:22:02,030 --> 00:22:04,410
including code the spacecraft actually needed to run.

364
00:22:05,810 --> 00:22:07,610
Nobody knows exactly why it failed.

365
00:22:08,170 --> 00:22:10,510
Maybe it simply wore out after 46 years.

366
00:22:10,510 --> 00:22:15,010
Maybe a single particle of cosmic radiation hit exactly the wrong spot.

367
00:22:16,550 --> 00:22:19,850
You cannot send a repair technician 15 billion miles.

368
00:22:20,310 --> 00:22:22,210
You cannot even send a replacement part.

369
00:22:22,630 --> 00:22:27,630
Whatever fixed this had to be a fix made entirely of new instructions sent over the radio.

370
00:22:29,390 --> 00:22:30,330
Voyager's project manager,

371
00:22:30,650 --> 00:22:34,530
Suzanne Dodd, put the real problem into words that stuck with a lot of people.

372
00:22:35,190 --> 00:22:37,830
The people that built the spacecraft are not alive anymore.

373
00:22:39,070 --> 00:22:42,290
There was no living engineer left to just call up and ask.

374
00:22:43,110 --> 00:22:47,690
Dodd described the process bluntly as an archaeology dig hunting through decades -old

375
00:22:47,690 --> 00:22:50,350
paper documentation that had never been digitized.

376
00:22:51,790 --> 00:22:55,730
Their solution was almost absurdly simple once someone thought of it.

377
00:22:56,190 --> 00:22:57,670
They couldn't fix the broken chip,

378
00:22:58,250 --> 00:23:02,490
so they split the code that used to live there into smaller pieces and hid those pieces

379
00:23:02,490 --> 00:23:04,550
in other parts of memory that still worked,

380
00:23:04,910 --> 00:23:08,550
rewriting every reference so the program would still run as one piece.

381
00:23:09,990 --> 00:23:15,210
On April 18, 2024, engineers sent up the first relocated block

382
00:23:15,210 --> 00:23:19,590
of code. Each command took over 22 hours just to arrive.

383
00:23:20,410 --> 00:23:21,510
Then they had to wait again,

384
00:23:21,810 --> 00:23:24,110
just as long for any answer at all.

385
00:23:25,650 --> 00:23:29,570
On April 20, 2024, the answer came back.

386
00:23:30,170 --> 00:23:34,650
Usable, readable engineering data for the first time in five months.

387
00:23:35,950 --> 00:23:40,930
By June of that year, full science data was flowing again from every instrument that

388
00:23:40,930 --> 00:23:45,530
was still switched on. A team that had never met the engineers who wrote the original

389
00:23:45,530 --> 00:23:50,590
code had just repaired a 1970s computer from 15 billion miles away

390
00:23:50,590 --> 00:23:53,990
using nothing but patience and 44 -hour round trips.

391
00:23:55,290 --> 00:23:56,890
It was a genuine reprieve,

392
00:23:57,250 --> 00:24:00,970
but it didn't touch the one problem nobody can ever patch with new code.

393
00:24:01,910 --> 00:24:07,250
Chapter 9, The Slow Goodbye The power problem was never

394
00:24:07,250 --> 00:24:09,610
a malfunction. It was always the plan,

395
00:24:09,970 --> 00:24:12,290
working exactly as physics predicted it would.

396
00:24:13,710 --> 00:24:18,490
Both spacecraft's plutonium generators lose roughly four watts of output every single

397
00:24:18,490 --> 00:24:21,490
year, permanently. By late 2023,

398
00:24:21,910 --> 00:24:24,510
Voyager 1 was down to about 225 watts,

399
00:24:24,790 --> 00:24:26,590
less than half of what it had at launch.

400
00:24:27,690 --> 00:24:29,230
There is no fix for that.

401
00:24:29,230 --> 00:24:33,290
There is only rationing. None of this is sentimental.

402
00:24:33,950 --> 00:24:36,770
The instruments still switched on are still returning real data.

403
00:24:36,910 --> 00:24:40,770
Nothing else in existence can collect direct measurements of the magnetic fields and

404
00:24:40,770 --> 00:24:42,690
particles of interstellar space itself.

405
00:24:44,090 --> 00:24:47,910
That's the actual reason engineers are fighting this hard over every last watt,

406
00:24:48,350 --> 00:24:50,430
not nostalgia. Science that,

407
00:24:50,430 --> 00:24:53,550
right now, only these two machines can still do.

408
00:24:54,750 --> 00:24:56,690
So, one instrument at a time,

409
00:24:57,090 --> 00:25:00,490
NASA has been permanently switching parts of both spacecraft off,

410
00:25:00,870 --> 00:25:02,790
on purpose, to keep something,

411
00:25:03,070 --> 00:25:04,870
anything, alive a little longer.

412
00:25:06,530 --> 00:25:09,810
Voyager 1's cosmic ray subsystem went dark on February 25,

413
00:25:10,250 --> 00:25:15,470
2025. Voyager 2's low -energy charged particle instrument went dark a month later,

414
00:25:15,770 --> 00:25:19,850
on March 24. Then, on April 17,

415
00:25:20,350 --> 00:25:25,670
2026, Voyager 1's own low -energy charged particle instrument went dark too.

416
00:25:26,070 --> 00:25:29,770
It had run almost continuously since launch in 1977,

417
00:25:30,470 --> 00:25:33,410
49 straight years gone in one command.

418
00:25:35,230 --> 00:25:37,990
Voyager 1 is now down to just two working instruments,

419
00:25:38,330 --> 00:25:42,870
a magnetometer and the plasma wave subsystem that first heard the boundary of interstellar

420
00:25:42,870 --> 00:25:46,870
space back in 2012. On February 27,

421
00:25:47,270 --> 00:25:52,390
2026, a routine roll maneuver caused a power dip that nearly tripped Voyager 1's own

422
00:25:52,390 --> 00:25:56,970
undervoltage protection, the safety system that shuts things down automatically before

423
00:25:56,970 --> 00:25:58,850
the spacecraft loses power completely.

424
00:26:00,250 --> 00:26:04,930
It didn't trip, but it was close enough that it changed how urgently engineers were treating

425
00:26:04,930 --> 00:26:07,770
the power problem. So this year,

426
00:26:08,070 --> 00:26:10,130
engineers tried something they'd never risked before,

427
00:26:10,370 --> 00:26:11,910
on a spacecraft this fragile.

428
00:26:13,550 --> 00:26:16,130
Internally, they call it the Big Bang Maneuver.

429
00:26:16,650 --> 00:26:18,350
Instead of just shutting an instrument off,

430
00:26:18,690 --> 00:26:22,910
they swapped groups of powered components for lower power alternatives all at once,

431
00:26:22,910 --> 00:26:25,650
while keeping the spacecraft just warm enough to keep working.

432
00:26:27,050 --> 00:26:28,830
They tested it on Voyager 2 first,

433
00:26:29,230 --> 00:26:31,710
in July of 2026. It worked.

434
00:26:32,630 --> 00:26:34,230
NASA confirmed it in early August.

435
00:26:34,710 --> 00:26:39,090
The maneuver bought Voyager 2's three remaining instruments roughly one more year of

436
00:26:39,090 --> 00:26:43,290
life. Without it, another one would have gone dark before the end of this year.

437
00:26:44,730 --> 00:26:46,910
A test of the same trick on Voyager 1,

438
00:26:47,210 --> 00:26:49,430
in late July, also went smoothly,

439
00:26:49,770 --> 00:26:51,270
according to the mission's own manager,

440
00:26:51,270 --> 00:26:56,330
Kareem Badarudin. There's even a real chance the instrument shut off in April

441
00:26:56,330 --> 00:26:58,630
could be switched back on if the fix holds.

442
00:26:59,130 --> 00:27:01,030
That has never happened before in this mission.

443
00:27:01,770 --> 00:27:03,570
Power has only ever gone one direction.

444
00:27:05,070 --> 00:27:06,790
None of this changes where this ends.

445
00:27:07,210 --> 00:27:11,630
It only changes when. NASA's own current estimate is that at least one instrument on

446
00:27:11,630 --> 00:27:14,390
each spacecraft can keep running into the 2030s.

447
00:27:15,630 --> 00:27:18,370
Even after every science instrument finally goes dark,

448
00:27:18,870 --> 00:27:20,790
NASA believes a bare engineering signal,

449
00:27:21,150 --> 00:27:22,810
no data, just a heartbeat,

450
00:27:23,130 --> 00:27:26,230
could keep reaching Earth's antennas until around 2036.

451
00:27:27,850 --> 00:27:30,410
After that, both spacecraft keep flying anyway.

452
00:27:31,410 --> 00:27:35,430
Silent forever, in their own independent orbit around the centre of the Milky Way,

453
00:27:35,670 --> 00:27:38,970
once every roughly 225 million years.

454
00:27:40,390 --> 00:27:44,250
NASA's own words for it, they will wander the galaxy for billions of years,

455
00:27:44,750 --> 00:27:46,690
longer than our own sun has left to burn.

456
00:27:48,190 --> 00:27:49,910
Still carrying that gold record.

457
00:27:50,690 --> 00:27:52,190
Still carrying Andruin's heartbeat.

458
00:27:53,030 --> 00:27:54,430
Long after everyone who built them,

459
00:27:54,750 --> 00:27:56,450
loved them, or listened to them,

460
00:27:56,670 --> 00:28:00,210
is gone. There's one more real thing,

461
00:28:00,450 --> 00:28:03,290
Voyager 1 left behind, and you can actually listen to it yourself.

462
00:28:04,730 --> 00:28:06,110
That plasma wave instrument,

463
00:28:06,410 --> 00:28:10,390
the same one that first detected the crossing into interstellar space in 2012,

464
00:28:11,050 --> 00:28:12,430
wasn't built to record sound.

465
00:28:13,170 --> 00:28:16,570
Space is a vacuum. There's nothing for sound to travel through.

466
00:28:17,510 --> 00:28:20,910
But it does measure oscillations in the plasma around the spacecraft.

467
00:28:21,630 --> 00:28:24,730
Vibrations that NASA can shift into the range of human hearing.

468
00:28:25,010 --> 00:28:28,070
The same way you'd slow down a back call so people can hear it.

469
00:28:29,390 --> 00:28:32,450
NASA released that recording publicly under a plain,

470
00:28:32,730 --> 00:28:36,370
honest title. Voyager captures sounds of interstellar space.

471
00:28:37,830 --> 00:28:40,430
It isn't music, it's data made audible.

472
00:28:41,050 --> 00:28:46,090
An eerie, rising drone, recorded 15 billion miles from every sound that has

473
00:28:46,090 --> 00:28:47,650
ever been heard by a human ear.

474
00:28:49,390 --> 00:28:51,250
Ed Stone took this job in 1972,

475
00:28:51,670 --> 00:28:53,810
and never really left it for 50 years.

476
00:28:54,330 --> 00:28:55,590
He never got to see it end,

477
00:28:55,710 --> 00:29:00,850
either. Nobody has yet. What stays with me about this story

478
00:29:00,850 --> 00:29:02,950
isn't the distance, it's the decision.

479
00:29:04,370 --> 00:29:06,750
Nobody is choosing to let Voyager die by accident.

480
00:29:07,470 --> 00:29:10,810
Every single shutdown is a person in a room on Earth,

481
00:29:11,010 --> 00:29:14,830
deciding exactly which part of a 49 -year -old machine gets to keep talking,

482
00:29:15,030 --> 00:29:16,610
and which part goes quiet first.

483
00:29:18,250 --> 00:29:19,810
It was built to last five years.

484
00:29:20,430 --> 00:29:22,570
Nobody who designed it is still running the mission.

485
00:29:23,270 --> 00:29:25,310
Almost nobody who built it is still alive.

486
00:29:25,810 --> 00:29:27,670
And it is still, right now,

487
00:29:28,070 --> 00:29:29,910
tonight, sending something home.

488
00:29:31,770 --> 00:29:33,850
Some day, the signal stops for good.

489
00:29:34,410 --> 00:29:35,770
Nobody knows the exact date.

490
00:29:36,230 --> 00:29:39,430
But the record keeps flying whether or not anyone is left listening.

491
00:29:40,990 --> 00:29:45,030
If a machine built for five years still running after 49,

492
00:29:45,650 --> 00:29:47,050
doesn't sound strange enough.

493
00:29:47,430 --> 00:29:50,830
Wait until you see the star astronomers just found orbiting Betelgeuse,

494
00:29:51,110 --> 00:29:53,150
hidden in plain sight for over a century.

495
00:29:55,150 --> 00:30:13,610
Subscribe to Mind Wired. Subscribe.

